From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 20:00:20 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] pad an emulated NV12 buffer's rows to the pitch Metal reads

An emulated NV12 or NV21 buffer is a byte image of the luma rows followed by
the interleaved chroma rows, as wide as the luma row. A chroma row holds a Cb
and a Cr byte for each pair of pixels, so of an odd width it is one byte longer
than the luma row and spills into the next row: a partial lock of a 333, 641 or
1921 pixel wide buffer wrote back chroma samples the next read did not find.
A row of 333 bytes is not an import pitch either.

Pad the image to a multiple of 16 bytes, the pitch Metal reads a texture over a
buffer at and the first one that holds every chroma row, and clamp the chroma
box of a partial transfer at the padded width so the Cr byte of the last pair
is sent with its Cb byte.
---
 virtgpu_virgl.c | 19 ++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/virtgpu_virgl.c b/virtgpu_virgl.c
index fcc50537c2a0165ce9da524fd2a16ca7dc7df1ae..df3b50217540c8aaf9281a6cac37e35531c058f5 100644
--- a/virtgpu_virgl.c
+++ b/virtgpu_virgl.c
@@ -143,6 +143,10 @@ static bool virgl_bitmask_supports_format(struct virgl_supported_format_mask *su
 	return supported->bitmask[bitmask_index] & (1 << bit_index);
 }
 
+// The row pitch Metal reads a texture over a buffer at is a multiple of 16 bytes, and the host
+// imports a buffer's memory into Vulkan at the pitch the guest reports; see virgl_native_stride.
+#define VIRGL_ROW_ALIGNMENT 16
+
 // The metadata generated here for emulated buffers is slightly different than the metadata
 // generated by drv_bo_from_format. In order to simplify transfers in the flush and invalidate
 // functions below, the emulated buffers are oversized. For example, ignoring stride alignment
@@ -212,7 +216,10 @@ static void virgl_get_emulated_metadata(const struct bo *bo, struct bo_metadata
 		y_plane_height = original_height;
 		c_plane_height = DIV_ROUND_UP(original_height, 2);
 
-		metadata->width = original_width;
+		// A chroma row holds a Cb and a Cr byte for each pair of pixels, so of an odd width it
+		// is one byte longer than the luma row. The rows are therefore padded to the pitch the
+		// host imports, which is also the first whole number of bytes that holds them.
+		metadata->width = ALIGN(original_width, VIRGL_ROW_ALIGNMENT);
 		metadata->height = y_plane_height + c_plane_height;
 
 		// Y-plane (full resolution)
@@ -337,8 +344,8 @@ static void virgl_get_emulated_transfers_params(const struct bo *bo,
 		// Cb and a Cr sample for each pair of pixels, and there is one for each pair of rows
 		uint32_t chroma_x = transfer_box->x & ~1u;
 		uint32_t chroma_end = DIV_ROUND_UP(transfer_box->x + transfer_box->width, 2) * 2;
-		if (chroma_end > bo->meta.width)
-			chroma_end = bo->meta.width;
+		if (chroma_end > ALIGN(bo->meta.width, VIRGL_ROW_ALIGNMENT))
+			chroma_end = ALIGN(bo->meta.width, VIRGL_ROW_ALIGNMENT);
 		xfer_params->xfer_boxes[1].x = chroma_x;
 		xfer_params->xfer_boxes[1].y = y_plane_height + transfer_box->y / 2;
 		xfer_params->xfer_boxes[1].width = chroma_end - chroma_x;
@@ -589,10 +596,8 @@ static uint32_t compute_virgl_bind_flags(uint64_t use_flags)
 // rows are padded to the first whole number of pixels that meets the alignment (2576 bytes), and
 // the renderer lays the texture's shared segment out by the same rule (vrend_renderer.c). The
 // buffer keeps its width: Android allows a stride wider than the width, and the host texture must
-// stay as wide as the guest samples it. A byte buffer has no rows, and a multi-planar format keeps
-// the layout its helpers define.
-#define VIRGL_ROW_ALIGNMENT 16
-
+// stay as wide as the guest samples it. A byte buffer has no rows, and the emulated multi-planar
+// formats pad theirs in virgl_get_emulated_metadata, where the host image is the bytes.
 static uint32_t virgl_native_stride(uint32_t format, uint32_t width, uint64_t use_flags)
 {
 	uint32_t stride = drv_stride_from_format(format, width, 0);
